# Erdős Problem #99 — Target A contact-edge lens shield

**Date:** 2026-09-05  
**Branch:** `research/target-c-c97-caterpillar-fan-capacity-2026-09-04`  
**Run type:** exact strengthening of Sections 169–173 / contracted Schur shield  
**Target:** locked-response contact separator  
**Status:** LEVEL I STILL OPEN

## 1. Main point

The promoted contact-shield argument deletes every response-lattice neighbor of every
contact-shield vertex.  For a response edge which actually crosses a unit contact
edge, this is much larger than necessary.

If `pq` is a unit contact and a strict response edge `xy` properly crosses `pq`, with
`x` a response point distinct from `p,q`, then the right endpoint `y` is not merely a
strict lattice neighbor of **one** of `p,q`: it is a strict common neighbor of **both**.

Consequently a contact separator can be shielded by the union of its edge lenses

`N_Lambda(p) cap N_Lambda(q)`

instead of the full vertex-neighborhood union.  Every unit-contact lens contains at
most two sites of the unit triangular lattice.

The resulting Schur penalty is

`|L_Sigma| + beta_Sigma`,

where `L_Sigma` is the right-shore lens union and `beta_Sigma` counts only basis
contact vertices whose fixed matched lattice partner was not already lens-shielded.
This is an exact basis-sensitive refinement of the previous `|R_Sigma|` penalty.

## 2. A crossing endpoint lies in both endpoint neighborhoods

Let `p,q,x` belong to one unit-separated response set, with

`|p-q|=1`,

`x notin {p,q}`,

and let `y` be a right response vertex satisfying

`|x-y|<1`.

Assume the closed segments `[x,y]` and `[p,q]` meet properly at `z`.
Unit separation gives

`|x-p|>=1`, `|x-q|>=1`.

By the triangle inequality through `z`,

`|x-p|+|y-q|`

`<= |x-z|+|z-p|+|y-z|+|z-q|`

`= |x-y|+|p-q| < 2`.

Therefore `|y-q|<1`.  Interchanging `p,q` gives `|y-p|<1`.

### Theorem EL1 — two-endpoint crossing transport

If a strict response radial edge from a response point distinct from the endpoints
properly crosses a unit contact edge `pq`, then

`y in N_Lambda(p) cap N_Lambda(q)`.

The endpoint-on-edge case is even simpler: if the right endpoint `y` lies in the
relative interior of `pq`, then both distances are strictly below one.  The collinear
overlap case is excluded exactly as in Lemma 173.1.  Thus the same conclusion covers
every non-vertex crossing relevant to the shielding argument.

This strictly strengthens the one-endpoint conclusion used in Section 169.5.

## 3. A unit-contact lens has at most two triangular-lattice sites

Fix `|p-q|=1` and put

`L(pq)=N_Lambda(p) cap N_Lambda(q)`.

After a rigid motion take

`p=(-1/2,0)`, `q=(1/2,0)`.

The open Euclidean lens

`B(p,1) cap B(q,1)`

is centrally symmetric about the origin.  For a point `(x,t)` in its closure the two
disk inequalities are equivalent to

`x^2+t^2+|x| <= 3/4`.

Hence its largest radius from the origin is `sqrt(3)/2`, attained only at the two
circle-intersection points `(0,+-sqrt(3)/2)`.  The open lens therefore has strict
diameter `<sqrt(3)`.

Distinct unit triangular-lattice sites have squared distances

`a^2+ab+b^2`, `a,b in Z`.

There is no value strictly between `1` and `3`: the equation
`a^2+ab+b^2=2` has no integer solution.  Thus any two distinct lattice sites in the
open lens must be exactly unit distance apart.

If the lens contained three lattice sites, all three would therefore form an elementary
unit equilateral triangle.  Both `p` and `q` would lie strictly inside all three unit
disks centered at those triangle vertices, i.e. in the interior of the corresponding
unit Reuleaux triangle.  That closed Reuleaux triangle has diameter one, and two
interior points have distance strictly below one.  This contradicts `|p-q|=1`.

### Theorem EL2 — two-site lens bound

For every unit contact edge,

`1 <= |L(pq)| <= 2`

under the generic response-lattice phase.  The lower bound is CN1; the upper bound is
the argument above.

## 4. Edge-lens geometric shield

Let `Sigma` be any finite unit-contact subgraph of `X^*`.  Define

`L_Sigma = (union_(pq in E(Sigma)) L(pq)) cap Y_in`.

Delete

`V(Sigma) sqcup L_Sigma`

from the full response graph.  A surviving response radial edge cannot pass through a
distinct contact vertex by the usual genericity condition.  If it meets the relative
interior of a contact edge, EL1 puts its right endpoint in `L_Sigma`, contradiction.
The collinear case is unchanged from Section 173.

### Theorem EL3 — lens-sector trapping

No response edge of

`G - (V(Sigma) sqcup L_Sigma)`

intersects `|Sigma|`.  Hence every surviving internal response component is contained
in one component of `K^*\|Sigma|`.

Moreover EL2 gives the crude but exact finite bound

`|L_Sigma| <= 2|E(Sigma)|`.

For the locked unit triangle core `T_*`, the three edge lenses have union of size at
most four.  Indeed a site in any edge lens is within unit distance of two of the three
triangle vertices.  Two such sites, even if attached to different triangle edges, are
both within unit distance of at least one common triangle vertex, because two
2-subsets of a 3-set intersect.  Thus the whole core lens union has strict diameter
`<2`.  Partitioning the triangular lattice into the four cosets of `2Lambda` gives

`|L_(T_*)|<=4`.

Consequently for the locked fan, whose unique cycle is `T_*` and whose remaining
`v-3` edges form channel trees,

`|L_Sigma| <= 4+2(v-3)=2v-2`.

## 5. Contracted gammoid penalty

Fix the Section-113 basis matching

`mu_0:B->Z`.

Write

`L_Z=L_Sigma cap Z`,

`L_O=L_Sigma cap O`.

In the contracted directed gammoid, shield

1. every contracted `B-Z` pair whose right endpoint lies in `L_Z`;
2. every contracted `B-Z` pair whose left endpoint lies in `B cap V(Sigma)`;
3. every terminal in `C^partial cap V(Sigma)`;
4. every sink in `L_O`.

The first two classes may overlap.  Define the uncovered basis-contact count

`beta_Sigma`

`= |{b in B cap V(Sigma): mu_0(b) notin L_Z}|`.

Then the number of shielded contracted basis pairs is exactly

`|L_Z|+beta_Sigma`.

Let `delta_Sigma^lens` be the same coarse sector Hall deficiency as in Section 173,
but formed after deleting the lens shield above.  EL3 supplies sector locality.
The standard path deletion argument therefore gives

### Theorem EL4 — edge-lens Schur nullity

`zeta >= delta_Sigma^lens - |L_Sigma| - beta_Sigma`.

The contact-terminal count cancels exactly, and deep nonbasis contact vertices never
occur internally on a directed Schur path.

The previous contact-neighborhood theorem has

`zeta >= delta_Sigma - |R_Sigma|`.

EL4 replaces that indiscriminate right-shore union by the strictly crossing-relevant
lens set, at the cost of only those basis contact vertices whose matched partner is not
already in a lens.

## 6. Explicit fan and crosscut forms

For a contact crosscut path with `v` vertices and `v-1` edges,

`|L_Sigma|+beta_Sigma <= 2(v-1)+|B cap V(Sigma)| <= 3v-2`.

For the locked connected unicyclic three-channel fan with `v` vertices,

`|L_Sigma|+beta_Sigma`

`<= (2v-2)+|B cap V(Sigma)|`

`<= 3v-2`.

The worst-case coefficient is still three because a response basis could in principle
contain every fan vertex.  The improvement is structural rather than merely a constant:
all right-shore shielding is now edge-local, and the remaining extra term is an exact
matroid/basis quantity `beta_Sigma`.

A closing continuation can attack either factor separately:

- prove that a suitable `Z`-basis matching makes `beta_Sigma` small for the
  stress-generated fan/crosscut; or
- use overlap among the two-site edge lenses to improve `|L_Sigma|` along the actual
  positive-stress geometry.

No Level I closure is claimed.